{"name":"Abalone","hex":"#f8f3f6","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"abalone","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An edible univalve mollusc of the genus Haliotis, having a shell lined with mother-of-pearl. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The meat of the aforementioned mollusc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/abalone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Abalone","blocks":[{"text":"Abalone are sea snails in the genus Haliotis, the only genus in the family Haliotidae. Abalone shells are distinctive for their flattened, ear-like shape, nacreous interior, and row of holes used for respiration. The flesh of abalone is widely considered to be a delicacy, and is consumed raw or cooked in a variety of cuisines. Abalone are globally distributed, with approximately 70 known species alive today. Though some species are small, the largest abalone can attain a length of 12 inches (300 mm)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/LivingAbalone.JPG/330px-LivingAbalone.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abalone"},{"title":"Abalone (board game)","blocks":[{"text":"Abalone is a two-player abstract strategy board game designed by Michel Lalet and Laurent Lévi in 1987. Players are represented by opposing black and white marbles on a hexagonal board with the objective of pushing six of the opponent's marbles off the edge of the board."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Abalone_Boardgame.jpg/330px-Abalone_Boardgame.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abalone_(board_game)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/AbaloneInside.jpg/500px-AbaloneInside.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/AbaloneInside.jpg/500px-AbaloneInside.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The iridescent surface inside a red abalone shell from Northern California. The US coin (quarter) is 24.3 mm or 0.955 in in diameter","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAbaloneInside.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Abalone_Santo_Andre_Povoa_de_Varzim.jpg/500px-Abalone_Santo_Andre_Povoa_de_Varzim.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Abalone_Santo_Andre_Povoa_de_Varzim.jpg/1280px-Abalone_Santo_Andre_Povoa_de_Varzim.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Abalone_Santo_Andre_Povoa_de_Varzim.jpg/500px-Abalone_Santo_Andre_Povoa_de_Varzim.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Abalone with a live sponge on its shell in Póvoa de Varzim, Portugal","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAbalone_Santo_Andre_Povoa_de_Varzim.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Abalone-farm1web.jpg/500px-Abalone-farm1web.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Abalone-farm1web.jpg/500px-Abalone-farm1web.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An abalone farm","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAbalone-farm1web.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Alaminosjf384.JPG/500px-Alaminosjf384.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Alaminosjf384.JPG/500px-Alaminosjf384.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Abalone hatchery"